{"story_id":"NO-8933-030","title":"Gertrude’s Bird","title_raw":"XXXI. GERTRUDE’S BIRD","notes":"","language":"en","original_language":"no","country":"Norway","country_code":"NO","culture_region":"ノルウェー","latitude":61.0,"longitude":9.0,"location_precision":"culture_region","map_location_type":"source_culture_region","collector":"Peter Christen Asbjørnsen; Jørgen Moe","translator":"George Webbe Dasent","publication_year":null,"year_evidence":"この電子版は版年を刻んでいない","book_id":"PG-8933","book_title":"Popular Tales from the Norse","source_provider":"Project Gutenberg","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8933","source_title":"Popular Tales from the Norse","license_status":"PUBLIC_DOMAIN_US","license_name":"Public Domain (United States)","license_jurisdiction":"US","rights_evidence_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/8933","redistribution_allowed":true,"commercial_use":true,"derivative_use":true,"ml_processing_status":"reviewed","verification_date":"2026-09-08","word_count":324,"text":"In those days when our Lord and St Peter wandered upon earth, they came\nonce to an old wife’s house, who sat baking. Her name was Gertrude, and\nshe had a red mutch on her head. They had walked a long way, and were\nboth hungry, and our Lord begged hard for a bannock to stay their\nhunger. Yes, they should have it. So she took a little tiny piece of\ndough and rolled it out, but as she rolled it, it grew and grew till it\ncovered the whole griddle.\n\nNay, that was too big; they couldn’t have that. So she took a tinier\nbit still; but when that was rolled out, it covered the whole griddle\njust the same, and that bannock was too big, she said; they couldn’t\nhave that either.\n\nThe third time she took a still tinier bit—so tiny you could scarce see\nit; but it was the same story over again—the bannock was too big.\n\n“Well”, said Gertrude, “I can’t give you anything; you must just go\nwithout, for all these bannocks are too big.”\n\nThen our Lord waxed wroth, and said:\n\n“Since you loved me so little as to grudge me a morsel of food, you\nshall have this punishment: you shall become a bird, and seek your food\nbetween bark and bole; and never get a drop to drink save when it\nrains.”\n\nHe had scarce said the last word before she was turned into a great\nblack woodpecker, or Gertrude’s bird, and flew from her kneading-trough\nright up the chimney; and till this very day you may see her flying\nabout, with her red mutch on her head, and her body all black, because\nof the soot in the chimney; and so she hacks and taps away at the trees\nfor her food, and whistles when rain is coming, for she is ever\nathirst, and then she looks for a drop to cool her tongue.","cluster":23,"analysis":{"analysis_version":"1.0","embedding_model":"intfloat/multilingual-e5-small","themes":[],"motifs":[],"animals":[{"label":"鳥","count":2,"method":"count","confidence":1.0}],"nature":[{"label":"海","count":2,"method":"count","confidence":1.0},{"label":"木","count":1,"method":"count","confidence":1.0}],"events":[{"position":0.0,"label":"Journey","hits":1,"lift":59.66,"method":"count"},{"position":0.091,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.182,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.273,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.364,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.455,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.545,"label":"Trial","hits":1,"lift":22.6,"method":"count"},{"position":0.636,"label":"Punishment","hits":1,"lift":345.03,"method":"count"},{"position":0.727,"label":"Transformation","hits":1,"lift":173.19,"method":"count"},{"position":0.818,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":0.909,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"},{"position":1.0,"label":null,"hits":0,"lift":0.0,"method":"count"}],"tension":[{"position":0.0,"tension":1.0,"raw":0.0,"method":"lexicon"},{"position":0.091,"tension":1.0,"raw":0.0,